design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:73: | Charging pads `pad_VBUS`, `pad_D-`, `pad_D+`, `pad_GND` | CP1, CP2, CP3, CP4 | gold-plated discs 1.6 mm across, 0.07 mm thick, soldered flat on the four FLEX2 pad lands with SAC305 paste (step 8), on the inside face, in the liner, order VBUS, D-, D+, GND (the parts list has no row for the disc yet) | `design/architecture.md:590`, `design/mech/thin/placement.csv:43` to `design/mech/thin/placement.csv:46` | 8 |
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:96: - **Join:** the board parts of `design/bom.csv` to PCB1 in TWO solder passes, one per face, each with its own paste stencil and its own reflow. The board has parts on both faces (`design/ecad/ring/pcb/board-notes-r3.md:35`). PASS 1, the SCREEN FACE (KiCad B.Cu, 21 parts in `design/ecad/ring/pcb/fab/ring-pos-back.csv:1`): J1 (`screen_connector`, Hirose FH35C-15S-0.3SHW(50), `design/bom.csv:11`), the eight 0201 parts C1, C2, C5, C7, C14, FB1, R71 and R72, with the stencil of `design/ecad/ring/pcb/fab/gerber/ring-B_Paste.gbr`. The 12 test pads TP1, TP3, TP4, TP5, TP6, TP12, TP14, TP15, TP17, TP18, TP19 and TP21 are copper on this face and get no paste [PROPOSED: reason, a solder bump on a test pad spoils the spring-pin contact of step 5; the file `ring-B_Paste.gbr` as delivered has a round 0.80 mm opening at each of the 12 test pads (`design/wiring/guide-fix-work/paste_count.log`), so the s
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:99: - **Check:** after PASS 1, optical inspection of the screen face at 10 times: J1 sits flat on the board with none of its 15 contact tails and its 2 mounting tails lifted, no solder bridge between neighbouring contacts (0.3 mm pitch), each of the eight 0201 parts in place and not tilted, no solder on any of the 12 test pads. After PASS 2, X-ray of U1 (61 balls), U2, U4 and the U3 lands; optical inspection of every other part of the parts face; then the screen face again: all 9 parts still in place, J1 still flat. A screen face part that fell in pass 2 is put back by hand soldering on that face (the ring is not reflowed a third time) [PROPOSED]. The number of reflow passes that J1 and the 0201 parts accept is not in any file cited here: the parts see two reflows (open, for the lead).
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:112: - **Join:** nothing is joined. Both faces of PCB1 are cleaned of flux residue, then dried: the SCREEN FACE round the 12 test pads, the PARTS FACE round the ribbon lands J_BAND_A and J_BAND_B [PROPOSED: reason, the spring pins of step 5 and the hot-bar joints of step 16 need bare copper; the paste and flux of step 2 are not named by any file]. History: this step fitted the light guide LG1 until v6; the light guide is removed in v7 (`design/architecture.md:834`).
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:131: - **Check:** each switch closes its own line and no other, measured with an ohmmeter on the ribbon's board end while pressing it; each switch also reads open (no connection) on its own line when it is released, so a switch that is closed all the time fails; height 0.53 mm; every switch base flat on the head (the model gives 0.0000 mm, `design/mech/thin/thin-notes.md:250`), checked with a feeler of 0.02 mm [PROPOSED].
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:136: - **Join:** M1 (`vibration_motor`, Jinlong W0525AB001D) to FLEX1, the red lead to the + land and the blue lead to the - land, by hand soldering (iron 320 C, 2 s) (`design/architecture.md:597`, `design/architecture.md:605`). FLEX1 has no mark on it. The + land is the land nearer the slot: land 1 of the motor lands, net VMOT, joined to land 7 (MOT+) of J_BAND_A. The - land is the other one: land 2, net GND, joined to land 6 (MOT-) of J_BAND_A (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:123`). The red lead is the + lead (`design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:24`).
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:138: - **Before:** step 6 passed. Cut each lead to the length of its ring size, measured from the motor body edge to the lead end: US 7 1.12 mm, US 8 1.07, US 9 1.08, US 10 1.14, US 11 1.39, US 12 1.44, US 13 1.39 (`design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:24`, `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:25`, `design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:125`). Strip 0.6 mm, tin the end, and lay it along the 0.80 mm side of its land (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:125`).
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:139: - **Check:** apply 3.0 V through a 200 mA current limit to the ribbon's board end, + on land 7 (VMOT) and - on land 6 (GND) of J_BAND_A (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:123`), for 0.5 s: the motor vibrates [PROPOSED: reason, the motor has its own drive chip, rated 3.0 V, start under 175 mA, `design/architecture.md:598`]. In the ring the same lines carry VMOT from U11, red lead on VMOT (`design/architecture.md:705`, `design/bom.csv:44`).
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:144: - **Join:** CP1 (`pad_VBUS`), CP2 (`pad_D-`), CP3 (`pad_D+`), CP4 (`pad_GND`) to the four pad lands of FLEX2 (`band_flex_away`). The pad is a gold-plated disc, 1.6 mm across and 0.07 mm thick, soldered flat on its land with SAC305 paste at this step. The bare land is NOT the pad: its ENIG gold would wear through under the case's spring pins (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:141`; `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:30` to `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:33`). Four discs, one per land, in the order VBUS (CP1), D- (CP2), D+ (CP3), GND (CP4). Each disc has a via at the centre of its land (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:141`). The disc is flush with the liner's inner face, as the model has it (`design/mech/thin/placement.csv:43`). Parts list: row CP1 to CP4 of `design/bom.csv` (no maker order code yet: a stamped, hard-gold plated disc to these dimensions).
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:145: - **How:** [PROPOSED: no design file names the method; the lead's workshop method of 2026-11-13, confirmed on the first ribbon.] 1. Lay the flat ribbon on a clean hot plate that is still cold, pad lands up. 2. Put one dot of SAC305 paste on each of the four lands with a dispensing needle. The dot is smaller than the land. 3. Lay one disc on each dot with tweezers, centred by eye under a 10 times lens. 4. Heat the plate until the paste turns bright and each disc settles flat (SAC305 melts at 217 to 220 C). 5. Lift the ribbon off the plate and let it cool in air. 6. Do not press a disc while the solder is liquid. This step comes before step 9, so the cell never sees this heat.
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:148: - **Check:** each disc lies flat on its land and is centred on it, with no tilt (a lifted edge fails); each disc is flush with the liner's inner face as the model says (`design/mech/thin/placement.csv:43`); continuity from each disc to its land at the ribbon's board end (VBUS to land 4, D- to land 5, D+ to land 6, GND to land 7 of J_BAND_B, `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:30` to `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:33`); no short between neighbouring discs or between their lands.
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:153: - **Join:** the three output tabs of the bought cell pack (BT1 with PRT1 (`cell_protection`) and RT1 inside it as one part, `design/bom.csv:48`; `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:34` to `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:36`) to the three lands of FLEX2, by hand soldering at low heat (iron 280 C at most, 2 s per joint). RT1 is not a separate part and has no joint of its own. Tab P+ goes on land 1 (VBAT), tab NTC on land 3 (NTC), tab P- on land 2 (GND). The tabs lie in the order P+, NTC, P- from the slot toward the cell, which is lands 1, 3, 2 (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:111` to `design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:115`) [PROPOSED: reason, the joint is 0.2 mm from the cell, `design/mech/thin/thin-notes.md:67`; the cell maker states the limit, S12].
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:156: - **Check:** the cell voltage appears on the cell lands at the ribbon's board end, positive on land 1 (VBAT) and negative on land 2 (GND): the meter's red probe on land 1 reads a positive voltage, and a negative reading means a tab is on the wrong land; the thermistor inside the pack reads about 10 k between land 3 (NTC) and land 2 (GND) at room temperature (`design/bom.csv:74`; `design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:113`, `design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:114`).
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:210: - **Join:** the board end of FLEX1 to J_BAND_A and of FLEX2 to J_BAND_B, on the board's parts face, by hot-bar soldering [PROPOSED: reason, a thin ribbon on a row of lands is joined in one press, and the lands got no paste in step 2]. The board with its screen is held above the open block, parts face up, while the ribbons rise through the slots (conflict K-A6). The model v5 keeps a free volume of 2.6 x 2.2 x 1.03 mm beside each slot under the board for one fold of the ribbon (`design/mech/thin/thin-notes.md:134`).
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:212: - **Before:** steps 14 and 15 passed. The insulating tape of step 9 is removed just before the joint.
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:213: - **Check:** once FLEX2 is joined, the battery voltage reaches the power chip. Above 2.75 V the chip powers up by itself (`design/datasheets/npm1304_datasheet_v1_0.txt:413`, `design/datasheets/npm1304_datasheet_v1_0.txt:1674`) and the ring boots to STANDBY with no VBUS (`design/firmware.md section 1.2, name STANDBY`). Proof: the jig's spring pins on CP1 to CP4 give USB; the ring enumerates and GET_INFO answers (`design/firmware.md section 6.3, name GET_INFO`). That proves the four pads, FLEX2 and J_BAND_B at once.
