design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:55: - The lands are bare copper with the finish of the board (ENIG or HASL are both accepted by a hot bar), solder mask opening 0.40 x 0.65 (0.05 per side). Pre-tin each land with a thin solder layer (stencil paste 0.05 mm or a tinned land), see section 7.
design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:65: The tail foot lies under the board with its OUTER face (F.Cu, the face with the lands) touching the board's lands. The tail board files are drawn looking at that face (x = u along the tail from the end under the board, y = +-v). Laid on the board the tail position (u, v) maps to the ring model position X = v - 2.5, Y = side x (3.4 + u), side = +1 for FLEX2 and -1 for FLEX1 (u grows toward the slot). Checked by working out the view from +Z: the transform has no mirror, so the pattern is NOT mirrored; the board footprint and the tail footprint use the same land numbers in the same places. check_band.py reads the pads of the delivered footprint J_BAND_x_BOARD (placed at X -2.250, Y side x 4.325) and the pads of the tail board, and requires each of the 8 and 7 lands to be within 0.05 mm and on the same net.
design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:91: Tail-end pattern (footprints "J_BAND_A_TAIL", "J_BAND_B_TAIL"): same land centres as section 2 seen on the tail; tail land 0.30 (along the tail) x 0.30 (across), bare ENIG, coverlay opening 0.40 x 0.40 (0.05 more per side than the land, so the land is NSMD). Tail frame: u = distance along the tail from the tail end that goes under the board (u = |Y| - 3.4 on the foot), v = X + 2.5. Foot outline: u 0 to 1.745, v -1.0 to +1.5 (X -3.5 to -1.0). Far row lands at u 0.475 (centre), near row at u 1.375, via row at u 0.925 (between the rows).
design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:94: - The tail stands vertical at |Y| 5.9 in the slot (thin.py line 66 `tail_y=5.9`). Above the floor it turns 90 degrees toward the board middle and lies flat under the board, outer face (F.Cu, the lands) up.
design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:106: - Solder: reflow, paste 0.08 thick (Alps spec slim_alps-skta-aaae010-orwin.pdf page 4, item 10.2 (4)), peak 260 C 3 s max, at most 2 reflows. No through hole under a switch (same page 10.2 (5)): the head vias sit in the lands, under the switch. Alps' item 10.2 (5) and D2 (page 7) warn about flux rising through holes when the back of a board is dip-soldered; here nothing is dip-soldered and the back of the head is closed by the polyimide stiffener (0.10, bonded on the back face), so no flux path exists. Held by: reflow only, stiffener on the back.
design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:115: - Tab order along the band, from the slot toward the cell: P+, NTC, P- (lands in that order 1, 3, 2). Tab material: nickel strip 0.30 wide x 0.10 thick (my requirement to the cell/PRT1 supplier; the model's tabs are 1.0 wide, thin.py line 735, they may be wider than the land, and the 0.30 strip is fully inside the land footprint 0.26 x 0.45).
design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:123: - Land 1 (pad 1) = red lead = M1 pin 1 = net VMOT = J_BAND_A land 7 (MOT+). Land 2 (pad 2) = blue lead = M1 pin 2 = net GND = J_BAND_A land 6 (MOT-) (netlist.csv rows M1, J_BAND_A 6 and 7; closes N7/R5 order; deferred item 46). Order along the tail from the slot: red first, then blue.
design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:137: Board lands pre-tinned (SAC305 stencil 0.05 mm). Tail foot placed on the lands (alignment by the 0.10 mm outline shown on the fixture, outline +-0.10 tolerance of the laser cut, JLCPCB flexible PCB capabilities page "FPC Outline"), coverlay openings 0.05 larger than the lands. Hot bar blade 3 mm wide (the foot is 2.5 wide, X -3.5 to -1.0, and 1.745 long) covering the whole foot, 260 C for 4 s at 20 N [my starting values: the Alps and the polyimide's limits are 260 C 3 s; the polyimide film is rated far above it]; the PI stiffener 0.10 on the back of the foot carries the pressure. Check after: continuity of each land to the next stage lands by the test pads (build guide), no bridge between the 0.21 mm gaps (land pitch 0.51 minus land 0.30).
design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:141: Closes N6, R9, deferred item 118. The copper land is a 1.6 mm disc on the inner face (B.Cu), ENIG (1 to 2 micro-inch gold, the only finish on the capability page). ENIG gold is a few hundredths of a micrometre thick and the case's spring pins touch it at every charge, so it would wear through. Decision: the pad that the case touches is a gold-plated disc, 1.6 dia x 0.07 thick (the model's pad, thin.py line 56 `cpad_t`), soldered flat on the land with SAC305 paste at assembly step 8 and set in the liner at step 21. The land is the joint, the disc is the wear surface. Position: disc centres on the flat tail at u = 26.01, 28.45, 30.89, 33.33 (US 9; per size in build_rows.json `pad_u`), pitch 2.438 on the flat (neutral axis, ribbon mid radius 9.62 against the pad face radius 9.475: 2.4 x 9.625/9.475 on the inner face), v = +1.5 (X -1.0), order VBUS, D-, D+, GND with rising angle (band-flex-t
design/ecad/case/pcb/board-notes.md:296: 4. Depth gauge, after the stakes are formed and before the main board ends are soldered (ring out of the case, wires hanging; source design/case/case-architecture.md line 219, "Builder's step (depth gauge, 0.01 mm digital, ring out, holder staked)"): with a 0.01 mm digital depth gauge measure how far each free plunger tip stands in front of the pin holder's front face, along the pin. The values are P2 and P3 1.170 mm, accepted within +-0.04 mm; P1 and P4 1.021 mm, accepted within +-0.03 mm (case-architecture.md line 219, from r4/pin_gauge.log, which I did not re-derive). A tip that stands out too little means the island sits too far back (pins too high): put stainless shim behind that island, between the island and the lug, in steps of 0.025 mm up to the shortfall (shim stock 0.025, 0.05 and 0.10 mm). A tip that stands out too much: lap the lug's face for that island on 600-grit paper on
design/ecad/case/pcb/board-notes.md:297: 5. Order inside each end: W4 (GND, black) first, then W2 and W3 (the twisted pair, W2 then W3), then W7 (yellow), and W1 (VRING, red) last (lead). Reason: ground goes on first so the board is tied to ground before any other wire is attached, and the supply wire W1 goes on last so that it is never a loose, partly soldered wire next to a signal wire while the others are still being placed (no part is powered during the build; this is the order only).
design/ecad/case/pcb/board-notes.md:299: 7. Main board end: the five holes are one column in the floor slot, in the order W1, W2, W7, W3, W4 (1.6 mm pitch). The bottom face of the main board carries the labels W1 +, W2 D-, W7 TCH, W3 D+ and W4 GND on the row of each hole (printed mirrored: read them with the board turned over). Put each wire through the hole of its label and check the colour against the label before soldering: red at "W1 +", white at "W2 D-", yellow at "W7 TCH", green at "W3 D+", black at "W4 GND". Solder in the order of step 5. The face on which the joint is made is not fixed by any file (the pads are plated through, `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv` "through hole,F.Cu,B.Cu"): solder on the face the wire stands out of (lead).
design/ecad/case/pcb/board-notes.md:300: 8. Check after soldering (case not powered and the cell lead W5 not yet soldered or the cell unplugged, so that nothing on the board is live; lead): with a multimeter on continuity, one probe on the plunger tip of each spring pin and the other on the main board pad: P1 to W1, P2 to W2, P3 to W3, P4 to W4 must all beep (a short, below about 50 ohm, lead); the touch strip E1 on the pin board front to W7 must beep. Then no continuity between any two of the five main board pads: the ten pairs W1-W2, W1-W3, W1-W4, W1-W7, W2-W3, W2-W4, W2-W7, W3-W4, W3-W7, W4-W7 must not beep. A reading of about 147 kohm between W1 and W4 is normal (R12 100 kohm plus R13 47 kohm from VRING through VRING_SENSE to ground, `design/ecad/case/netlist.csv`); a beep is a short. A wire that fails is reheated or replaced before the pin board goes on.
